"The Tutte‐Grothendieck Group of an Alphabetic Rewriting System - Poinsot - 2013 - International Scholarly Research Notices - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2013/574578)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.8 years ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Integral Temperley-Lieb Algebra ... This alphabetic rewriting system is easily shown to be convergent. ... We recover the fact that the n-th Temperley-Lieb ℤ-algebra (with parameter 1) ℤ〈Y〉/I, where I the two-sided ideal generated by the following relations x_{i}x_{j} = x_{j}x_{i} for |i − j | > 1, x_{i}x_{j}x_{i} = x_{i} for |i − j | = 1, for 1 ≤ i ≤ n − 1 is free with basis Y.\n#### 4.3.3. Integral Temperley-Lieb Algebra\n\nLet n ∈ ℕ such that n > 2. Let us consider Y = {x_{1}, …, x_{n−1}}. Let θ = {(x_{i},x_{j}):|i − j | > 1}∪{(x_{j}, x_{i}):|i − j | > 1}. Let X = 𝒮(Y, θ). Let R = {(ux_{i}x_{j}x_{i}v, ux_{i}v):|i − j | = 1, u,. This alphabetic rewriting system is easily shown to be convergent. The corresponding Tutte-Grothendieck ring is ℤ^{(Y)}. We recover the fact that the n-th Temperley-Lieb ℤ-algebra (with parameter 1) ℤ〈Y〉/I, where I the two-sided ideal generated by the following relations x_{i}x_{j} = x_{j}x_{i} for |i − j | > 1, x_{i}x_{j}x_{i} = x_{i} for |i − j | = 1, for 1 ≤ i ≤ n − 1 is free with basis Y.\n\n#### 4.3.4. The Poincaré-Birkhoff-Witt Theorem\n\nLet 𝔤 be a Lie algebra over ℤ which is free as an ℤ-module (see [19]). Let ℬ be a basis of 𝔤 seen as a (free) ℤ-module. Let us assume that ℬ is linearly ordered by ≤. Let X = ℬ^{*} be the free monoid generated by ℬ. Let R = {(uhgv, ughv) : g, h ∈ ℬ, g < h, u, v ∈ ℬ^{*}}⊆X × X^{⊕}. It is obvious that (X^{⊕}, ⇒_{R}) is a convergent alphabetic rewriting system. Moreover, Irr(X) = {g_{1} ⋯ g_{n} : n ≥ 0, g_{i} ∈ ℬ     for all     0 ≤ i ≤ n, g_{i} ≤ g_{i+1}    for  all    0 ≤ i < n} and its Tutte-Grotendieck group is ℤ^{(Irr(X))}, while its universal Tutte-Grothendieck invariant t is the reordering of an element of X in an increasing order (relative to ≤). We recognize the famous Poincaré-Birkhoff-Witt theorem ([20–22]) since the universal enveloping ℤ-algebra of 𝔤 over ℤ is free with basis Irr(X).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch for a basis of the Temperley-Lieb algebra, using rewriting systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19360)\nciteturn1academia11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Title: Search for a basis of the Temperley-Lieb algebra, using rewriting systems\nAuthors: Julien Thiebaut\nTitle: Search for a basis of the Temperley-Lieb algebra, using rewriting systems\nAuthors: Julien Thiebaut\nDate: Tue Aug 26 18:36:59 2025\n\nWe begin by defining Temperley-Lieb algebra, in two different ways: as a presented algebra or as a diagrammatic algebra. Next, we look for a basis algorithmically, using rewriting theory. Finally, we introduce a generalization of the Temperley-Lieb algebra, which is an oriented version of the previous one. This pushes us to employ a more efficient tool, category theory, to use rewriting to easily obtain a basis for the algebra.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstrained Flips in Plane Spanning Trees (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol357-gd2025/LIPIcs.GD.2025.5/LIPIcs.GD.2025.5.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Besides showing that the happy edge property does not hold for rotations, we improve the previous ... Related Version arXiv Version: https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15520 [2]\nfixed-parameter tractable algorithm to compute the shortest compatible flip sequence between two\ngiven trees. (2) Rotations are flips in which the removed and inserted edge share a common vertex.\nBesides showing that the happy edge property does not hold for rotations, we improve the previous\nupper bound of 2n −O(1) for the diameter of the rotation graph to 7n\n4 −O(1).\n2012 ACM Subject Classification Mathematics of computing →Discrete mathematics; Mathematics\nof computing →Combinatorics; Mathematics of computing →Combinatoric problems\nKeywords and phrases Non-crossing spanning trees, Flip Graphs, Diameter, Complexity, Happy\nedges\nDigital Object Identifier 10.4230/LIPIcs.GD.2025.5\nRelated Version arXiv Version: https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15520 [2]\nFunding Joseph Dorfer: Austrian Science Foundation (FWF) 10.55776/DOC183.\n1\nIntroduction\nLet S be a finite point set in the plane in general position, that is, no three points lie on a\ncommon line. We call S a convex point set if no point in S lies in the interior of the convex\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-08-11&page=197&range=182)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Aug 05 2025 math.CO math.OC arXiv:2508.02108v1\n\nFrançois Clément, Dan Guyer\n\nAug 05 2025 math.CO math.OC arXiv:2508.02108v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2508.02108, author = {François Clément and Dan Guyer}, title = {{M}onotone {P}aths on {A}cyclic 3-{R}egular {G}raphs}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2508.02108}, note = {arXiv:2508.02108v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nMotivated by trying to understand the behavior of the simplex method, Athanasiadis, De Loera and Zhang provided upper and lower bounds on the number of the monotone paths on 3-polytopes. For simple 3-polytopes with $2n$ vertices, they showed that the number of monotone paths is bounded above by $(1+\\varphi)^n$, with $\\varphi$ being the golden ratio. We improve the result and show that for a larger family of graphs the number is bounded above by $c \\cdot 1.6779^n$ for some universal constant $c$. Meanwhile, the best known construction and conjectured extremizer is approximately $\\varphi^n$.\n\n  * AutoLoRA: Automatic LoRA Retrieval and Fine-Grained Gated Fusion for Text-to-Image Generation\n\nZhiwen Li, Zhongjie D